[[:db/cas 17592186493917 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the output voltage of a randomly selected AC adapter. Then we have $X∼N(19,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>19.3).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>20)=0.01.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20) & =1-P(X>20) \\\\  & =1-0.01 \\\\  & =0.99\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20) & =0.99 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{20-19}{\\sigma }) & =0.99 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.99 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.99)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.99)≈2.3263.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =2.3263 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{2.32639} \\\\  & ≈0.42985.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X>19.3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>19.3) & =1-P(X\\le 19.3) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le \\frac{19.3-19}{0.42985}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z\\le 0.70) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.70) \\\\  & =1-0.7580 \\\\  & ≈24.2%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $24.2%$ of AC adapters have a voltage greater than $19.3$ volts." "Let $X$ represent the output voltage of a randomly selected AC adapter. Then we have $X∼N(19,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>19.3).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>20)=0.01.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20) & =1-P(X>20) \\\\  & =1-0.01 \\\\  & =0.99\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20) & =0.99 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{20-19}{\\sigma }) & =0.99 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.99 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.99)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.99)≈2.3263.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =2.3263 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{2.32639} \\\\  & ≈0.42985.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X>19.3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>19.3) & =1-P(X\\le 19.3) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le \\frac{19.3-19}{0.42985}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z\\le 0.70) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.70) \\\\  & =1-0.7580 \\\\  & ≈24.2\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $24.2\\%$ of AC adapters have a voltage greater than $19.3$ volts."] [:db/cas 17592186493962 :question/problem "The lengths of babies at birth in a given population are normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{cm}$ and a standard deviation of $6\\,\\text{cm}.$ Additionally, the length of $60%$ of the newborns falls between $0.9\\mu \\,\\text{cm}$ and $1.1\\mu \\,\\text{cm}.$ Find the mean $\\mu $ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |" "The lengths of babies at birth in a given population are normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{cm}$ and a standard deviation of $6\\,\\text{cm}.$ Additionally, the length of $60\\%$ of the newborns falls between $0.9\\mu \\,\\text{cm}$ and $1.1\\mu \\,\\text{cm}.$ Find the mean $\\mu $ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186493967 :question/problem "A volleyball school for children splits the players into three teams, team $1$, team $2,$ and team $3.$ \n\n- $44%$ of children are in team $1,$ and $23%$ of children in this team have been playing for over two years.\n\n- $32%$ of children are in team $2,$ and $26%$ of children in this team have been playing for over two years.\n\n- $24%$ of children are in team $3.$\n\nIf $7%$ of all children can play in the blocker position, $40%$ of children who can play as blockers have been playing for over two years, and $10%$ of children who have been playing for over two years can play as blockers, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child in team $3$ has been playing for over two years? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "A volleyball school for children splits the players into three teams, team $1$, team $2,$ and team $3.$ \n\n- $44\\%$ of children are in team $1,$ and $23\\%$ of children in this team have been playing for over two years.\n\n- $32\\%$ of children are in team $2,$ and $26\\%$ of children in this team have been playing for over two years.\n\n- $24\\%$ of children are in team $3.$\n\nIf $7\\%$ of all children can play in the blocker position, $40\\%$ of children who can play as blockers have been playing for over two years, and $10\\%$ of children who have been playing for over two years can play as blockers, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child in team $3$ has been playing for over two years? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186493967 :question/worked-solution "Let $B$ be the event that a child can play in the blocker position, $I$ be the event that a child has been playing for over two years, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a child is in team $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.44 &  & P(I|{T}_{1})=0.23 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.32 &  & P(I|{T}_{2})=0.26 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.44+0.32+0.24=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $7%$ of children can play in the blocker position, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.07.\n$$\n\n- Since $40%$ of children who can play as blockers have been playing for over two years, we have\n\n$$\nP(I|B)=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of children who have been playing for over two years can play as blockers, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|I)=0.1.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child in team 3 has been playing for over two years, which is represented as $P(I|{T}_{3}).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|I) & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I)} \\\\ P(B|I) & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(I|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(I|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\ 0.1 & =\\frac{(0.4)(0.07)}{(0.23)(0.44)+(0.26)(0.32)+P(I|{T}_{3})(0.24)} \\\\ 0.1 & =\\frac{0.028}{0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(I|{T}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.1(0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3})) & =0.028 \\\\ 0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.028}{0.1} \\\\ 0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{7}{25} \\\\ 0.24P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{7}{25}-0.1844 \\\\ P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.24}(\\frac{7}{25}-0.1844) \\\\ P(I|{T}_{3}) & ≈0.398\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "Let $B$ be the event that a child can play in the blocker position, $I$ be the event that a child has been playing for over two years, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a child is in team $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.44 &  & P(I|{T}_{1})=0.23 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.32 &  & P(I|{T}_{2})=0.26 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.44+0.32+0.24=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $7\\%$ of children can play in the blocker position, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.07.\n$$\n\n- Since $40\\%$ of children who can play as blockers have been playing for over two years, we have\n\n$$\nP(I|B)=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since $10\\%$ of children who have been playing for over two years can play as blockers, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|I)=0.1.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child in team 3 has been playing for over two years, which is represented as $P(I|{T}_{3}).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|I) & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I)} \\\\ P(B|I) & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(I|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(I|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\ 0.1 & =\\frac{(0.4)(0.07)}{(0.23)(0.44)+(0.26)(0.32)+P(I|{T}_{3})(0.24)} \\\\ 0.1 & =\\frac{0.028}{0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(I|{T}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.1(0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3})) & =0.028 \\\\ 0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.028}{0.1} \\\\ 0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{7}{25} \\\\ 0.24P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{7}{25}-0.1844 \\\\ P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.24}(\\frac{7}{25}-0.1844) \\\\ P(I|{T}_{3}) & ≈0.398\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186493970 :question/problem "In a distribution center, the average weight of shipped packages is $2.5$ kilograms. However, $20%$ of packages weigh no more than $2.3$ kilograms. Assuming that package weights are normally distributed, find the standard deviation, $\\sigma ,$ of the package weights, **rounded to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$kg$" "In a distribution center, the average weight of shipped packages is $2.5$ kilograms. However, $20\\%$ of packages weigh no more than $2.3$ kilograms. Assuming that package weights are normally distributed, find the standard deviation, $\\sigma ,$ of the package weights, **rounded to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$kg$"] [:db/cas 17592186493975 :question/problem "The average amount of coffee dispensed into medium cups is $10$ ounces. Additionally, in $15%$ of cups, the coffee amount is less than $9.1$ ounces. Assuming the amount dispensed is normally distributed, find the percentage of cups containing less than $8.5$ ounces of coffee. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $\\,p\\,$ | $0.01$ | $0.03$ | $0.05$ | $0.10$ | $0.15$ | $0.20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z).$*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ | $\\,0.0336\\,$ | $\\,0.0329\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ | $\\,0.0418\\,$ | $\\,0.0409\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.0526\\,$ | $\\,0.0516\\,$ | $\\,0.0505\\,$ |\n\nApprximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of cups contain less than $8.5$ ounces of coffee." "The average amount of coffee dispensed into medium cups is $10$ ounces. Additionally, in $15\\%$ of cups, the coffee amount is less than $9.1$ ounces. Assuming the amount dispensed is normally distributed, find the percentage of cups containing less than $8.5$ ounces of coffee. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $\\,p\\,$ | $0.01$ | $0.03$ | $0.05$ | $0.10$ | $0.15$ | $0.20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z).$*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ | $\\,0.0336\\,$ | $\\,0.0329\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ | $\\,0.0418\\,$ | $\\,0.0409\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.0526\\,$ | $\\,0.0516\\,$ | $\\,0.0505\\,$ |\n\nApprximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of cups contain less than $8.5$ ounces of coffee."] [:db/cas 17592186493975 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the amount of coffee in a randomly selected cup. Then we have $X∼N(10,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<8.5).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nWe know that $P(X<9.1)=0.15.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<9.1) & =0.15 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{9.1-10}{\\sigma }) & =0.15 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.15 \\\\ \\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.15).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.15)=-1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma } & =-1.0364 \\\\ \\frac{0.9}{\\sigma } & =1.0364 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{0.9}{1.0364} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈0.8684.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<8.5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<8.5) & =P(X\\le 8.5) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{8.5-10}{0.8684}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.73) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.73) \\\\  & =0.0418 \\\\  & ≈4.18%.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $4.18%$ of coffee cups contain less than $8.5$ ounces of coffee." "Let $X$ represent the amount of coffee in a randomly selected cup. Then we have $X∼N(10,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<8.5).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nWe know that $P(X<9.1)=0.15.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<9.1) & =0.15 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{9.1-10}{\\sigma }) & =0.15 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.15 \\\\ \\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.15).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.15)=-1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma } & =-1.0364 \\\\ \\frac{0.9}{\\sigma } & =1.0364 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{0.9}{1.0364} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈0.8684.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<8.5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<8.5) & =P(X\\le 8.5) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{8.5-10}{0.8684}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.73) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.73) \\\\  & =0.0418 \\\\  & ≈4.18\\%.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $4.18\\%$ of coffee cups contain less than $8.5$ ounces of coffee."] [:db/cas 17592186495051 :question/problem "On average, $53%$ of the emails Emily receives are spam. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that she has $70$ or more spam emails in her inbox if she has $140$ emails in her inbox. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2420\\,$ | $\\,0.2389\\,$ | $\\,0.2358\\,$ |" "On average, $53\\%$ of the emails Emily receives are spam. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that she has $70$ or more spam emails in her inbox if she has $140$ emails in her inbox. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2420\\,$ | $\\,0.2389\\,$ | $\\,0.2358\\,$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186495053 :question/problem "A $3$-pound bag of flour costs $\\[math] 6.42.$ What is the price per pound?" "A $3$-pound bag of flour costs $\\$ 6.42.$ What is the price per pound?"] [:db/cas 17592186495053 :question/worked-solution "To find the unit price, we divide the total cost by the number of pounds:\n\n$$\n6.42  \\div 3 = 2.14\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit price is $\\[math] 2.14$ per pound." "To find the unit price, we divide the total cost by the number of pounds:\n\n$$\n6.42  \\div 3 = 2.14\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit price is $\\$ 2.14$ per pound."] [:db/cas 17592186495054 :question/problem "At a gourmet shop, a $4$-cup container of artisanal soup costs $$11.20.$ What's the unit price per fluid ounce?\n\nAssume that there are $8$ fluid ounces in one cup." "At a gourmet shop, a $4$-cup container of artisanal soup costs $\\$11.20.$ What's the unit price per fluid ounce?\n\nAssume that there are $8$ fluid ounces in one cup."] [:db/cas 17592186495054 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert from cups to fluid ounces. There are $8$ fluid ounces in each cup, so we multiply the number of cups by $8:$  \n\n$$\n4\\times 8=32\n$$\n\nSo, $4$ cups is the same as $32$ fluid ounces. Now, to find the unit price per fluid ounce, we divide the total price by $32:$  \n\n$$\n11.20÷32=0.35\n$$\n\nTherefore, the artisanal soup costs $$0.35$ per fluid ounce." "First, we convert from cups to fluid ounces. There are $8$ fluid ounces in each cup, so we multiply the number of cups by $8:$  \n\n$$\n4\\times 8=32\n$$\n\nSo, $4$ cups is the same as $32$ fluid ounces. Now, to find the unit price per fluid ounce, we divide the total price by $32:$  \n\n$$\n11.20÷32=0.35\n$$\n\nTherefore, the artisanal soup costs $\\$0.35$ per fluid ounce."] [:db/cas 17592186495061 :question/problem "A bottle contains $32$ fluid ounces of juice and costs $\\[math]8.00.$ What's the unit price per cup?\n\nAssume that there are $8$ fluid ounces in one cup." "A bottle contains $32$ fluid ounces of juice and costs $\\$8.00.$ What's the unit price per cup?\n\nAssume that there are $8$ fluid ounces in one cup."] [:db/cas 17592186495061 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert from fluid ounces to cups. There are $8$ fluid ounces in each cup, so we divide the number of fluid ounces by $8\\mathbin{:}$  \n\n$$\n32 \\div 8 = 4\n$$\n\nSo, $32$ fluid ounces is the same as $4$ cups.  \n\nNow, to find the unit price