</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        {{field-1}}\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"35px\">\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        {{field-2}}\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$1$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n        {{field-3}}\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"35px\">\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$0$\n      </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> {{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"] [:db/cas 17592186497331 :question/problem "The following table shows a proportional relationship between the weight $w$ (in pounds) of apples purchased at a particular supermarket and the total cost $c$ (in dollars) that customers must pay. \n\n| Weight ($w$) | Cost ($c$) |\n| --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ |\n| $4$ | $6$ |\n| $8$ | $12$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The unit rate is  $$3$ per pound.\n\nII. One pound of apples costs $$1.25.$\n\nIII. The cost of $5$ pounds of apples is $$7.50.$" "The following table shows a proportional relationship between the weight $w$ (in pounds) of apples purchased at a particular supermarket and the total cost $c$ (in dollars) that customers must pay. \n\n| Weight ($w$) | Cost ($c$) |\n| --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ |\n| $4$ | $6$ |\n| $8$ | $12$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The unit rate is  $\\$3$ per pound.\n\nII. One pound of apples costs $\\$1.25.$\n\nIII. The cost of $5$ pounds of apples is $\\$7.50.$"] [:db/cas 17592186497331 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $w$ and $c$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nTo find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(w,c)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(2,3),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table.  \n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality as follows: \n\n$$\nk=\\frac{c}{w}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe units of $k$ are \"dollars per pound\" since we divided the cost $c$ by the weight $w.$ \nTherefore, the relationship between $c$ and $w$ is \n\n$$\nc=\\frac{3}{2}w.\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, with that in mind, let's look at each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. We know that $k=\\frac{3}{2},$ which means that the unit rate is $\\frac{3}{2}$ dollars per pound.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $k=\\frac{3}{2}=1.5,$ one pound of apples costs $$1.50.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, if we substitute $w=5$ into the relationship $c=\\frac{3}{2}w,$ we get \n\n$$\nc=\\frac{3}{2}(5)=7.5\n$$\n\nwhich means that the cost of $5$ pounds of apples is $$7.50.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "We're told that $w$ and $c$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nTo find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(w,c)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(2,3),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table.  \n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality as follows: \n\n$$\nk=\\frac{c}{w}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe units of $k$ are \"dollars per pound\" since we divided the cost $c$ by the weight $w.$ \nTherefore, the relationship between $c$ and $w$ is \n\n$$\nc=\\frac{3}{2}w.\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, with that in mind, let's look at each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. We know that $k=\\frac{3}{2},$ which means that the unit rate is $\\frac{3}{2}$ dollars per pound.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $k=\\frac{3}{2}=1.5,$ one pound of apples costs $\\$1.50.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, if we substitute $w=5$ into the relationship $c=\\frac{3}{2}w,$ we get \n\n$$\nc=\\frac{3}{2}(5)=7.5\n$$\n\nwhich means that the cost of $5$ pounds of apples is $\\$7.50.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186497341 :question/problem "A fungi culture increases by $30%$ each week. If the culture started with $5$ fungi, what equation should we solve to determine how many weeks will it take for the fungi culture to increase to $100?$" "A fungi culture increases by $30\\%$ each week. If the culture started with $5$ fungi, what equation should we solve to determine how many weeks will it take for the fungi culture to increase to $100?$"] [:db/cas 17592186497341 :question/worked-solution "The fungi culture $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $30%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=5$ is the initial number of fungi.\n\n- $r=0.3$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in weeks, since the culture started.\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =5\\cdot (1+0.3)^{t} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (1.3)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the number of weeks it takes for the fungi culture to increase to $100,$ we would need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =100 \\\\ 5\\cdot (1.3)^{t} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The fungi culture $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $30\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=5$ is the initial number of fungi.\n\n- $r=0.3$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in weeks, since the culture started.\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =5\\cdot (1+0.3)^{t} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (1.3)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the number of weeks it takes for the fungi culture to increase to $100,$ we would need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =100 \\\\ 5\\cdot (1.3)^{t} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186497363 :question/problem "Paul is conducting a scientific experiment with amoebas. He notices that the number of amoebas grows by  $65%$ each day. If there were initially $140$ amoebas, complete the following equation that we should solve to determine how long it will take for the number of amoebas to increase to $800.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,(${{field-2}}$)^{t}=800$" "Paul is conducting a scientific experiment with amoebas. He notices that the number of amoebas grows by  $65\\%$ each day. If there were initially $140$ amoebas, complete the following equation that we should solve to determine how long it will take for the number of amoebas to increase to $800.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,(${{field-2}}$)^{t}=800$"] [:db/cas 17592186497363 :question/worked-solution "The amoeba population $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $65%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=140$ is the initial number of amoebas.\n\n- $r=0.65$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in days, since the beginning of the observation.\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =140\\cdot (1+0.65)^{t} \\\\  & =140\\cdot (1.65)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the time it will take for the amoeba population to increase to $800,$ we would need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =800 \\\\ 140\\cdot (1.65)^{t} & =800.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The amoeba population $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $65\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=140$ is the initial number of amoebas.\n\n- $r=0.65$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in days, since the beginning of the observation.\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =140\\cdot (1+0.65)^{t} \\\\  & =140\\cdot (1.65)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the time it will take for the amoeba population to increase to $800,$ we would need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =800 \\\\ 140\\cdot (1.65)^{t} & =800.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186497370 :question/problem "An economic study in Chile showed that its gross domestic product (GDP) increases fourfold each decade. If the GDP was $$0.5$ billion in $2010$, how many decades will it take for the GDP to increase to $$512$ billion?" "An economic study in Chile showed that its gross domestic product (GDP) increases fourfold each decade. If the GDP was $\\$0.5$ billion in $2010$, how many decades will it take for the GDP to increase to $\\$512$ billion?"] [:db/cas 17592186497370 :question/worked-solution "We know that the GDP increases fourfold each decade. So the GDP, which we'll call $y(t),$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $4.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=0.5$ billion dollars was the GDP in $2010.$\n\n- $b=4$ is the growth factor.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in decades, since $2010.$\n\nTherefore, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=0.5\\cdot {4}^{t}.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the time it takes for the GDP to increase to $$512$ billion, we need to solve the equation $y(t)=512$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =512 \\\\ 0.5\\cdot {4}^{t} & =512 \\\\ {4}^{t} & =\\frac{512}{0.5} \\\\ {4}^{t} & =1\\,024 \\\\ {4}^{t} & ={4}^{5} \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, it will take $5$ decades for the GDP to increase to $$512$ billion." "We know that the GDP increases fourfold each decade. So the GDP, which we'll call $y(t),$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $4.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=0.5$ billion dollars was the GDP in $2010.$\n\n- $b=4$ is the growth factor.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in decades, since $2010.$\n\nTherefore, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=0.5\\cdot {4}^{t}.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the time it takes for the GDP to increase to $\\$512$ billion, we need to solve the equation $y(t)=512$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =512 \\\\ 0.5\\cdot {4}^{t} & =512 \\\\ {4}^{t} & =\\frac{512}{0.5} \\\\ {4}^{t} & =1\\,024 \\\\ {4}^{t} & ={4}^{5} \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, it will take $5$ decades for the GDP to increase to $\\$512$ billion."] [:db/cas 17592186497382 :question/problem "The profits of a store $P(t),$ $t$ years after $2018,$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\cdot (1.1)^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ was the profit in $2018.$ If the profits in $2020$ were of $$48\\,400,$ what was the profit in $2018?$" "The profits of a store $P(t),$ $t$ years after $2018,$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\cdot (1.1)^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ was the profit in $2018.$ If the profits in $2020$ were of $\\$48\\,400,$ what was the profit in $2018?$"] [:db/cas 17592186497382 :question/worked-solution "The store profits in $2020$ were of $$48\\,400,$ this is $2$ years after $2018.$ Thus, $P(2)=48\\,400.$\n\nTo find the profit ${P}_{0}$ in $2018,$  we substitute $t=2$ and $P(2)=48\\,400$ into the given equation and solve for ${P}_{0},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & ={P}_{0}\\cdot (1.1)^{2} \\\\ 48\\,400 & ={P}_{0}\\cdot (1.1)^{2} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{48\\,400}{1.21} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =40\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profits in $2018$ were of $$40\\,000.$" "The store profits in $2020$ were of $\\$48\\,400,$ this is $2$ years after $2018.$ Thus, $P(2)=48\\,400.$\n\nTo find the profit ${P}_{0}$ in $2018,$  we substitute $t=2$ and $P(2)=48\\,400$ into the given equation and solve for ${P}_{0},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & ={P}_{0}\\cdot (1.1)^{2} \\\\ 48\\,400 & ={P}_{0}\\cdot (1.1)^{2} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{48\\,400}{1.21} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =40\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profits in $2018$ were of $\\$40\\,000.$"] [:db/cas 17592186498501 :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83