00$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.3\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{0.4}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\ 2.3\\pm 0.206\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499553 :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=81$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =6.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-15,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=81$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =6.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-15,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*"] [:db/cas 17592186499553 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=81\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.10\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{81}} \\\\ -15\\pm 1.097\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=81\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.10\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{81}} \\\\ -15\\pm 1.097\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186500286 :question/problem "![](images/69/693576483310a4656435ea4a3d8d9652b8de563d26a87c7c80fb902a3bef1e29.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=14$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=3$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=16$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=9$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=7,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" "![](images/69/693576483310a4656435ea4a3d8d9652b8de563d26a87c7c80fb902a3bef1e29.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=14$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=3$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=16$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=9$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=7,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*"] [:db/cas 17592186500286 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=9-7=2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{3}^{2}}{14}+\\frac{{5}^{2}}{16}}≈2.911\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2\\pm 2.911\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=9-7=2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{3}^{2}}{14}+\\frac{{5}^{2}}{16}}≈2.911\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2\\pm 2.911\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186500299 :question/problem "In $1990$, there were $5,000$ cell phone subscribers in a city. The number of subscribers increased by $35%$ per year until $2005.$ How many cell-phone subscribers were there in $2005?$" "In $1990$, there were $5,000$ cell phone subscribers in a city. The number of subscribers increased by $35\\%$ per year until $2005.$ How many cell-phone subscribers were there in $2005?$"] [:db/cas 17592186500299 :question/worked-solution "The number of subscribers $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $35%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=5\\,000$ is the initial number of subscribers.\n\n- $r=0.35$ is the growth rate ($35%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\,000\\cdot (1+0.35)^{t} \\\\  & =5\\,000\\cdot (1.35)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the expression for the number of subscribers after $15$ years, we get\n\n$$\ny(15)=5,000\\cdot (1.35)^{15}≈450\\,792.\n$$\n\nWe round to the nearest whole number because we are talking about people." "The number of subscribers $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $35\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=5\\,000$ is the initial number of subscribers.\n\n- $r=0.35$ is the growth rate ($35\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\,000\\cdot (1+0.35)^{t} \\\\  & =5\\,000\\cdot (1.35)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the expression for the number of subscribers after $15$ years, we get\n\n$$\ny(15)=5,000\\cdot (1.35)^{15}≈450\\,792.\n$$\n\nWe round to the nearest whole number because we are talking about people."] [:db/cas 17592186500301 :question/problem "It is known that the weights of oranges from farms X and Y are normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=7$ grams and ${\\sigma }_{y}=8$ grams, respectively. The owner of the farms wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the weights of oranges from farm X is smaller than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the weights of oranges from farm Y. He decides to construct a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nThe farmer sampled $25$ oranges from farm X and $20$ oranges from farm Y, finding that their mean weights are $125$ grams and $128.2$ grams, respectively. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $-3.2\\pm 3.737.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $90%$ confidence interval are negative.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" "It is known that the weights of oranges from farms X and Y are normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=7$ grams and ${\\sigma }_{y}=8$ grams, respectively. The owner of the farms wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the weights of oranges from farm X is smaller than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the weights of oranges from farm Y. He decides to construct a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nThe farmer sampled $25$ oranges from farm X and $20$ oranges from farm Y, finding that their mean weights are $125$ grams and $128.2$ grams, respectively. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $-3.2\\pm 3.737.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $90\\%$ confidence interval are negative.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186500301 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=125-128.2=-3.2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{7}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{8}^{2}}{20}}≈3.737\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -3.2\\pm 3.737\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $-3.2\\pm 3.737.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The confidence interval contains $0:$ \n\n$$\n(-3.2-3.737,\\,-3.2+3.737)=(-6.937,\\,0.537)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that ${\\mu }_{x}={\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ 