llar.\n\nThe approximate price of each can of paint is $\n{{field-1}}." "To complete another job, Mike bought $4$ identical cans of paint, paying a total of $\\$95.64.$ Estimate the price of each can of paint by rounding the amount of money to the nearest dollar.\n\nThe approximate price of each can of paint is \\$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186500333 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the price of each can of paint, we need to find an approximation for $95.64÷4.$\n\nSo, we round the dividend to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n95.64\\to 96\n$$\n\nNow, we divide $96$ by $4$ as usual:\n\n$$\n96÷4=24\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximate price of each can of paint is $$24.$" "To estimate the price of each can of paint, we need to find an approximation for $95.64÷4.$\n\nSo, we round the dividend to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n95.64\\to 96\n$$\n\nNow, we divide $96$ by $4$ as usual:\n\n$$\n96÷4=24\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximate price of each can of paint is $\\$24.$"] [:db/cas 17592186500337 :question/problem "The number of sales of a new brand of cellphone varies inversely with its price, in dollars. A store sells $100$ units of this new brand of cellphone when its price is $$700.$ What is the constant of variation?" "The number of sales of a new brand of cellphone varies inversely with its price, in dollars. A store sells $100$ units of this new brand of cellphone when its price is $\\$700.$ What is the constant of variation?"] [:db/cas 17592186500337 :question/worked-solution "The number of sales $n$ is inversely proportional to the price $P,$ so we have\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{k}{P},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when its price is $$700,$ the store sells $100$ units. We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =\\frac{k}{700} \\\\ 100\\cdot 700 & =k \\\\ 70\\,000 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k=70\\,000.$" "The number of sales $n$ is inversely proportional to the price $P,$ so we have\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{k}{P},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when its price is $\\$700,$ the store sells $100$ units. We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =\\frac{k}{700} \\\\ 100\\cdot 700 & =k \\\\ 70\\,000 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k=70\\,000.$"] [:db/cas 17592186500340 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=128$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=7.2$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=49$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=53$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=52,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=128$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=7.2$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=49$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=53$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=52,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*"] [:db/cas 17592186500340 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=53-52=1\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{7.2}^{2}}{128}+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{49}}≈1.676\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 1\\pm 1.676\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=53-52=1\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{7.2}^{2}}{128}+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{49}}≈1.676\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 1\\pm 1.676\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186500344 :question/problem "The weights of two age groups of men, X and Y, are normally distributed with a standard deviation of  ${\\sigma }_{x}=10\\,\\text{kg},$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=12\\,\\text{kg},$ respectively.\nA researcher wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the men's weights in group X is less than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the men's weights in group Y. He decides to construct a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nThe researcher sampled $28$ men from group X and $30$ men from group Y, finding that their mean weights are $74\\,kg$ and $82\\,kg,$ respectively. Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. The end-points of the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $-8\\pm 4.005.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $99%$ confidence interval are negative.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ 