\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=31-37=-6\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5}^{2}}{98}+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{63}}≈1.038\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -6\\pm 1.038\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186500361 :question/problem "Mark's expenditure of money $E$, in dollars, is inversely proportional to his savings $S,$ also in dollars. If Mark spends $$500$ when he saves $$200,$ what will be Mark's expenditure (to the nearest dollar) when he saves $$300?$" "Mark's expenditure of money $E$, in dollars, is inversely proportional to his savings $S,$ also in dollars. If Mark spends $\\$500$ when he saves $\\$200,$ what will be Mark's expenditure (to the nearest dollar) when he saves $\\$300?$"] [:db/cas 17592186500361 :question/worked-solution "Mark's expenditure of money $E$ is inversely proportional to his savings $S,$ so we have\n\n$$\nE=\\frac{k}{S},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that Mark spends $$500$ when he saves $$200.$ We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\begin{aligned}500 & =\\frac{k}{200} \\\\ 500\\cdot 200 & =k \\\\ 100\\,000 & =k\\end{aligned}\n\n$$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nE=\\frac{100\\,000}{S}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find Mark's expenditure when he saves $$300,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nE=\\frac{100\\,000}{300}≈333\n$$\n\nTherefore,  Mark's expenditure when  $S=$300$ is $E=$333.$" "Mark's expenditure of money $E$ is inversely proportional to his savings $S,$ so we have\n\n$$\nE=\\frac{k}{S},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that Mark spends $\\$500$ when he saves $\\$200.$ We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\begin{aligned}500 & =\\frac{k}{200} \\\\ 500\\cdot 200 & =k \\\\ 100\\,000 & =k\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nE=\\frac{100\\,000}{S}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find Mark's expenditure when he saves $\\$300,$ as follows:\n\n\nE=\\frac{100\\,000}{300}≈333\n\n\nTherefore,  Mark's expenditure when  $S=\\$300$ is $E=\\$333.$"] [:db/cas 17592186500363 :question/problem "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=25$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=21$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=9.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=15$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=16,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=25$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=21$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=9.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=15$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=16,$ find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*"] [:db/cas 17592186500363 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=15-16=-1\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{10}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{9}^{2}}{21}}≈7.221\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\pm 7.221\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=15-16=-1\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{10}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{9}^{2}}{21}}≈7.221\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\pm 7.221\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186500368 :question/problem "It is known that sales (quantities) of a new product in two different states X and Y are normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=7$, respectively. A marketing agency wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of sales in state X is greater than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of sales in state Y. They decide to construct a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the two population means.\n\nThe agency sampled $14$ shops from state X and $15$ shops from state Y, finding that their mean sales were $54.5$ and $48.6$, respectively. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $5.9\\pm 7.630.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $99%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" "It is known that sales (quantities) of a new product in two different states X and Y are normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=7$, respectively. A marketing agency wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of sales in state X is greater than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of sales in state Y. They decide to construct a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the two population means.\n\nThe agency sampled $14$ shops from state X and $15$ shops from state Y, finding that their mean sales were $54.5$ and $48.6$, respectively. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $5.9\\pm 7.630.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $99\\%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186500368 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=54.5-48.6=5.9\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\n \n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5}^{2}}{14}+\\frac{{7}^{2}}{15}}≈5.790\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 5.9\\pm 5.790\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The end-points of our confidence interval are $5.9\\pm 5.790.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are positive:\n\n$$\n(5.9-5.790,\\,5.9+5.790)=(0.11,\\,11.69)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are positive, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=54.5-48.6=5.9\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\n \n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5}^{2}}{14}+\\frac{{7}^{2}}{15}}≈5.790\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 5.9\\pm 5.790\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The end-points of our confidence interval are $5.9\\pm 5.790.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are positive:\n\n$$\n(5.9-5.790,\\,5.9+5.790)=(0.11,\\,11.69)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are positive, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186500378 :question/problem "A watch collector bought a watch for $$50.$ They estimate that the value of the watch increases at a rate of $8%$ every decade. According to this estimate, what will be the price of the watch after $3$ decades? Round your answer to the nearest dollar." "A watch collector bought a watch for $\\$50.$ They estimate that the value of the watch increases at a rate of $8\\%$ every decade. According to this estimate, what will be the price of the watch after $3$ decades? Round your answer to the nearest dollar."] [:db/cas 17592186500378 :question/worked-solution "The price of the watch $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $8%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=50$ dollars is the original price of the watch.\n\n- $r=0.08$ is the growth rate ($8%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in decades.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =50\\cdot (1+0.08)^{t} \\\\  & =50\\cdot (1.08)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the function at $t=3$ gives the value of the watch after $3$ decades.\n\n$$\ny(3)=50\\cdot (1.08)^{3}≈63\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the watch after $3$ decades will be approximately $$63.$" "The price of the watch $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $8\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=50$ dollars is the original price of the watch.\n\n- $r=0.08$ is the growth rate ($8\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in decades.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =50\\cdot (1+0.08)^{t} \\\\  & =50\\cdot (1.08)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the function at $t=3$ gives the value of the watch after $3$ decades.\n\n$$\ny(3)=50\\cdot (1.08)^{3}≈63\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the watch after $3$ decades will be approximately $\\$63.$"] [:db/cas 17592186501162 :question/problem "If $\\,\\boxed{}\\times 25%=90,$ then what is the missing number?" "If $\\,\\boxed{}\\times 25\\%=90,$ then what is the missing number?"] [:db/cas 17592186501162 :question/worked-solution "The part is $90,$ the percentage is $25%,$ and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\n \n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $25%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{100}=\\frac{25÷25}{100÷25}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =90÷25% \\\\  & =90÷\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =90\\times \\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =90\\times 4 \\\\  & =360\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The part is $90,$ the percentage is $25\\%,$ and we need to 