tly, how many problems were on the test?" "On a math test, Kevin answered $95\\%$ of the problems correctly. If he answered $38$ problems correctly, how many problems were on the test?"] [:db/cas 17592186501191 :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a percentage, and we need to find the whole.\n\nIn this case, the part is the number of problems answered correctly, which is $38.$ The percentage is $95%,$ and the whole is the total number of problems on the test. \n\nTo solve this question, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $95%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{95}{100}=\\frac{95÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{19}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =38÷95% \\\\  & =38÷\\frac{19}{20} \\\\  & =38\\times \\frac{20}{19} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{19}\\times 20 \\\\  & =2\\times 20 \\\\  & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there were $40$ problems on the test." "We're given a part and a percentage, and we need to find the whole.\n\nIn this case, the part is the number of problems answered correctly, which is $38.$ The percentage is $95\\%,$ and the whole is the total number of problems on the test. \n\nTo solve this question, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $95\\%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{95}{100}=\\frac{95÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{19}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =38÷95\\% \\\\  & =38÷\\frac{19}{20} \\\\  & =38\\times \\frac{20}{19} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{19}\\times 20 \\\\  & =2\\times 20 \\\\  & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there were $40$ problems on the test."] [:db/cas 17592186501207 :question/problem "What is the missing number?\n\n{{field-1}}$\\times \\,30%=24.$" "What is the missing number?\n\n{{field-1}}$\\times \\,30\\%=24.$"] [:db/cas 17592186501207 :question/worked-solution "The part is $24,$ the percentage is $30%,$ and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $30%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{100}=\\frac{30÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =24÷30% \\\\  & =24÷\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =24\\times \\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{3}\\times 10 \\\\  & =8\\times 10 \\\\  & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $80\\times 30%=24.$" "The part is $24,$ the percentage is $30\\%,$ and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $30\\%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{100}=\\frac{30÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =24÷30\\% \\\\  & =24÷\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =24\\times \\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{3}\\times 10 \\\\  & =8\\times 10 \\\\  & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $80\\times 30\\%=24.$"] [:db/cas 17592186501210 :question/problem "A school's softball team lost $18%$ of the games they played last year. If the team lost $9$ games last year, how many games did the team win?" "A school's softball team lost $18\\%$ of the games they played last year. If the team lost $9$ games last year, how many games did the team win?"] [:db/cas 17592186501210 :question/worked-solution "We need to know the total number of games played to determine the number of games won. \n\nWe're given a part and a percentage, and we need to find the whole.\n\nIn this case, the part is the number of games the team lost, which is $9.$ The percentage is $18%,$ and the whole is the total number of games played. \n\nTo find the whole, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $18%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{18}{100}=\\frac{18÷2}{100÷2}=\\frac{9}{50}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =9÷18% \\\\  & =9÷\\frac{9}{50} \\\\  & =9\\times \\frac{50}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}\\times 50 \\\\  & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total number of games played by the softball team last year was $50.$ The total number of games won is\n\n$$\n50-9=41.\n$$\n\nHence, the softball team won $41$ games last year." "We need to know the total number of games played to determine the number of games won. \n\nWe're given a part and a percentage, and we need to find the whole.\n\nIn this case, the part is the number of games the team lost, which is $9.$ The percentage is $18\\%,$ and the whole is the total number of games played. \n\nTo find the whole, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $18\\%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{18}{100}=\\frac{18÷2}{100÷2}=\\frac{9}{50}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =9÷18\\% \\\\  & =9÷\\frac{9}{50} \\\\  & =9\\times \\frac{50}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}\\times 50 \\\\  & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total number of games played by the softball team last year was $50.$ The total number of games won is\n\n$$\n50-9=41.\n$$\n\nHence, the softball team won $41$ games last year."] [:db/cas 17592186501211 :question/problem "$14%$ of{{field-1}}\nis $7.$" "$14\\%$ of{{field-1}}\nis $7.$"] [:db/cas 17592186501211 :question/worked-solution "The part i