,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ |" "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=99$ from a population where $45\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that less than $50\\%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186502078 :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=52$ from a population of size $N=120$ in which $8%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=4$ from a population of size $N=200$ in which $70%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=20$ from a population of size $N=500$ in which $70%$ of the population has a particular characteristic." "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=52$ from a population of size $N=120$ in which $8\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=4$ from a population of size $N=200$ in which $70\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=20$ from a population of size $N=500$ in which $70\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic."] [:db/cas 17592186502085 :question/problem "It is known that $35%$ of the people arriving at a clothing store buy something. If the manager randomly samples $65$ customers, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $13$ but fewer than $26$ of them bought something. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" "It is known that $35\\%$ of the people arriving at a clothing store buy something. If the manager randomly samples $65$ customers, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $13$ but fewer than $26$ of them bought something. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*"] [:db/cas 17592186502086 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=16$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $5.4$ and the sample standard deviation is $8,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.947)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $15$ degrees of freedom.*" "Consider a sample of size $n=16$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $5.4$ and the sample standard deviation is $8,$ find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.947)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $15$ degrees of freedom.*"] [:db/cas 17592186502086 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}-{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\\le \\mu \\le \\overset{―}{x}+{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=5.4,\\,n=16,\\,s=8.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.947)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{15,0.005}=2.947.\n$$\n\n![](images/bb/bbaa00deb18e38f1b201317615a790764519ad5d2c5b436fe13044a09c71beac.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5.4 & \\pm 2.947\\cdot \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\ 5.4 & \\pm 5.894\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[