e of size $n=200$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $90\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=40$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $80\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=30$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $70\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic."] [:db/cas 17592186502116 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=256$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $105,$ and the sample standard deviation is $36,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.969)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $255$ degrees of freedom.*" "Consider a sample of size $n=256$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $105,$ and the sample standard deviation is $36,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.969)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $255$ degrees of freedom.*"] [:db/cas 17592186502116 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=256\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=105,\\,n=256,\\,s=36.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.969)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{255,0.025}=1.969.\n$$\n\n![](images/d1/d108130264501813e8bfed332f345d8d005095127c5d9d8e169a1c2ae83b2ec7.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}105 & \\pm 1.969\\cdot \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{256}} \\\\ 105 & \\pm 4.430\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=256\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=105,\\,n=256,\\,s=36.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.969)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{255,0.025}=1.969.\n$$\n\n![](images/d1/d108130264501813e8bfed332f345d8d005095127c5d9d8e169a1c2ae83b2ec7.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}105 & \\pm 1.969\\cdot \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{256}} \\\\ 105 & \\pm 4.430\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502117 :question/problem "An amusement park knows that $25%$ of its visitors ride the rollercoaster. The manager of the park conducts a random sample of $125$ visitors. Find a normal approximation for the probability that fewer than $40$ of them rode the rollercoaster. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" "An amusement park knows that $25\\%$ of its visitors ride the rollercoaster. The manager of the park conducts a random sample of $125$ visitors. Find a normal approximation for the probability that fewer than $40$ of them rode the rollercoaster. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*"] [:db/cas 17592186502118 :question/problem "A random sample of $100$ students from a particular university yields a mean GPA of $3.1$ with a sample standard deviation of $0.48.$ Find a  $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean GPA $\\mu $ of all students.  \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $99$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $100$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $101$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.625\\,$ |" "A random sample of $100$ students from a particular university yields a mean GPA of $3.1$ with a sample standard deviation of $0.48.$ Find a  $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean GPA $\\mu $ of all students.  \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $99$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $100$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $101$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.625\\,$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186502118 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=100\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=3.1,\\,n=100,\\,s=0.48.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{99,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{99,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=100-1=99$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/1b/1bb4e66b68bbc1fb55a36abafb4f184fb6b25b16314e989a47fe6b3a835cea65.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{99,0.05}=1.660:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $99$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $1.660$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $100$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $101$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.625\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.1 & \\pm 1.66\\cdot \\frac{0.48}{\\sqrt{100}}\\,\\text{grade points} \\\\ 3.1 & \\pm 0.08\\text{ grade points}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=100\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=3.1,\\,n=100,\\,s=0.48.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{99,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{99,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=100-1=99$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/1b/1bb4e66b68bbc1fb55a36abafb4f184fb6b25b16314e989a47fe6b3a835cea65.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{99,0.05}=1.660:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $99$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $1.660$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $100$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $101$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.625\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.1 & \\pm 1.66\\cdot \\frac{0.48}{\\sqrt{100}}\\,\\text{grade points} \\\\ 3.1 & \\pm 0.08\\text{ grade points}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502123 :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=50$ from a population where $20%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that between $15%$ and $25%$ of the sample have the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=50$ from a population where $20\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that between $15\\%$ and $25\\%$ of the sample have the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*"] [:db/cas 17592186502131 :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=76$ from a population where $24%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $30%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8849\\,$ | $\\,0.8869\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9032\\,$ | $\\,0.9049\\,$ | $\\,0.9066\\,$ | $\\,0.9082\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9192\\,$ | $\\,0.9207\\,$ | $\\,0.9222\\,$ | $\\,0.9236\\,$ |" "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=76$ from a population where $24\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $30\\%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8849\\,$ | $\\,0.8869\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9032\\,$ | $\\,0.9049\\,$ | $\\,0.9066\\,$ | $\\,0.9082\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9192\\,$ | $\\,0.9207\\,$ | $\\,0.9222\\,$ | $\\,0.9236\\,$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186502132 :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $50$ recently caught shrimps, where $55%$ of the shrimps in the sea weigh more than $10\\,\\text{g}.$\n\nII. A sample of $27$ companies, where $82%$ of the companies in the country employ more than $100$ people.\n\nIII. A sample of $100$ songs in a streaming service, given that $92%$ of the songs last less than $3$ minutes.\n\n*Hint: In each case, you may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $50$ recently caught shrimps, where $55\\%$ of the shrimps in the sea weigh more than $10\\,\\text{g}.$\n\nII. A sample of $27$ companies, where $82\\%$ of the companies in the country employ more than $100$ people.\n\nIII. A sample of $100$ songs in a streaming service, given that $92\\%$ of the songs last less than $3$ minutes.\n\n*Hint: In each case, you may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [:db/cas 17592186502132 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50(0.55) \\\\  & =27.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50(1-0.55) \\\\  & =22.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =27(0.82) \\\\  & =22.14 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =27(1-0.82) \\\\  & =4.86 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100(0.92) \\\\  & =92 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100(1-0.92) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50(0.55) \\\\  & =27.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50(1-0.55) \\\\  & =22.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =27(0.82) \\\\  & =22.14 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =27(1-0.82) \\\\  & =4.86 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100(0.92) \\\\  & =92 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100(1-0.92) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186502134 :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c9b1e09734e74c098fd5e91eb3c72079157c3a69b1bb1d039bb46e823e5b7b6.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=25$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $35.5,$ and the sample standard deviation is $3,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.064)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $24$ degrees of freedom,  as shown in the diagram above.*" "![](images/4c/4c9b1e09734e74c098fd5e91eb3c72079157c3a69b1bb1d039bb46e823e5b7b6.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=25$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $35.5,$ and the sample standard deviation is $3,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.064)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $24$ degrees of freedom,  as shown in the diagram above.*"] [:db/cas 17592186502134 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom. \n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=35.5,\\,n=25,\\,s=3.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.064)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{24,0.025}=2.064.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}35.5 & \\pm 2.064\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\ 35.5 & \\pm 1.238\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom. \n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=35.5,\\,n=25,\\,s=3.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.064)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{24,0.025}=2.064.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}35.5 & \\pm 2.064\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\ 35.5 & \\pm 1.238\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502135 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=400$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $57,$ and the sample standard deviation is $26,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.649)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $399$ degrees of freedom.*" "Consider a sample of size $n=400$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $57,$ and the sample standard deviation is $26,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.649)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $399$ degrees of freedom.*"] [:db/cas 17592186502135 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=400\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=57,\\,n=400,\\,s=26.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.649)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{399,0.05}=1.649.\n$$\n\n![](images/7d/7d2cc71801ad3826dddfb670518ad038ccbf11f363569896fe3c3b673e5c58a6.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}57 & \\pm 1.649\\cdot \\frac{26}{\\sqrt{400}} \\\\ 57 & \\pm 2.144\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=400\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=57,\\,n=400,\\,s=26.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.649)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{399,0.05}=1.649.\n$$\n\n![](images/7d/7d2cc71801ad3826dddfb670518ad038ccbf11f363569896fe3c3b673e5c58a6.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}57 & \\pm 1.649\\cdot \\frac{26}{\\sqrt{400}} \\\\ 57 & \\pm 2.144\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502139 :question/problem "A sample of the different prices of a given product in $64$ randomly selected stores in a given state yields a mean price of $$37.15$ with a sample standard deviation of $$2.00.$  Find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean price of the product  $\\mu $ across the state. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $62$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.670\\,$ | $\\,1.999\\,$ | $\\,2.388\\,$ | $\\,2.657\\,$ |\n| $63$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.669\\,$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.387\\,$ | $\\,2.656\\,$ |\n| $64$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.669\\,$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.386\\,$ | $\\,2.655\\,$ |" "A sample of the different prices of a given product in $64$ randomly selected stores in a given state yields a mean price of $\\$37.15$ with a sample standard deviation of $\\$2.00.$  Find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean price of the product  $\\mu $ across the state. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $62$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.670\\,$ | $\\,1.999\\,$ | $\\,2.388\\,$ | $\\,2.657\\,$ |\n| $63$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.669\\,$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.387\\,$ | $\\,2.656\\,$ |\n| $64$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.669\\,$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.386\\,$ | $\\,2.655\\,$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186502139 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=64\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=$37.15,\\,n=64,\\,s=$2.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{99,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{63,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=64-1=63$ degrees of freedom. \n\n![](images/43/43ddb73bfc30887b0de7b7debf98407dbcea330ebe55c485982d5c05c0681c9a.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{63,0.05}=1.669:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $62$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.670\\,$ | $\\,1.999\\,$ | $\\,2.388\\,$ | $\\,2.657\\,$ |\n| $63$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $1.669$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.387\\,$ | $\\,2.656\\,$ |\n| $64$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.669\\,$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.386\\,$ | $\\,2.655\\,$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}37.15 & \\pm 1.669\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{64}}\\text{ dollars} \\\\ 37.15 & \\pm 0.417\\text{ dollars}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=64\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=$37.15,\\,n=64,\\,s=$2.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{99,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{63,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=64-1=63$ degrees of freedom. \n\n![](images/43/43ddb73bfc30887b0de7b7debf98407dbcea330ebe55c485982d5c05c0681c9a.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{63,0.05}=1.669:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $62$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.670\\,$ | $\\,1.999\\,$ | $\\,2.388\\,$ | $\\,2.657\\,$ |\n| $63$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $1.669$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.387\\,$ | $\\,2.656\\,$ |\n| $64$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.669\\,$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.386\\,$ | $\\,2.655\\,$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}37.15 & \\pm 1.669\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{64}}\\text{ dollars} \\\\ 37.15 & \\pm 0.417\\text{ dollars}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502147 :question/problem "Over $6$ days, $$14.70$ is deducted from a transit card balance due to daily fares of equal size. What is the change in the balance each day?" "Over $6$ days, $\\$14.70$ is deducted from a transit card balance due to daily fares of equal size. What is the change in the balance each day?"] [:db/cas 17592186502147 :question/worked-solution "Since the balance decreases by $$14.70$ over $6$ days, the total change is $-$14.70.$\n\nThis change occurs evenly over $6$ days, so we divide the total change by $6:$\n\n$$\n-14.70÷6\n$$\n\nEvaluating the quotient, we get\n\n$$\n-14.70÷6=-2.45.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in the balance each day is $-$2.45.$" "Since the balance decreases by $\\$14.70$ over $6$ days, the total change is $-\\$14.70.$\n\nThis change occurs evenly over $6$ days, so we divide the total change by $6:$\n\n$$\n-14.70÷6\n$$\n\nEvaluating the quotient, we get\n\n$$\n-14.70÷6=-2.45.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in the balance each day is $-\\$2.45.$"] [:db/cas 17592186502149 :question/problem "The body mass index (BMI) of Dr. Lee's patients is normally distributed. A random sample of $9$ patients yields a mean BMI of $25.5\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}$ with a standard deviation of $2.6\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}.$ Find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean BMI $\\mu $ among all of Dr. Lee's patients. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $\\,1.860\\,$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $\\,3.355\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |" "The body mass index (BMI) of Dr. Lee's patients is normally distributed. A random sample of $9$ patients yields a mean BMI of $25.5\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}$ with a standard deviation of $2.6\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}.$ Find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean BMI $\\mu $ among all of Dr. Lee's patients. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $\\,1.860\\,$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $\\,3.355\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186502149 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=25.5\\,\\,n=9,\\,s=2.6\\,.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{8,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{8,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=9-1=8$ degrees of freedom. \n\n![](images/ce/ce7d5fc9bbf2c9e28a0862247e6f762cad8a718401828070e96041e7f82b1891.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{8,0.05}=1.860:$   \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $1.860$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $\\,3.355\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 25.5\\pm 1.860\\cdot \\frac{2.6}{\\sqrt{9}}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2} \\\\  & 25.5\\pm 1.612\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=25.5\\,\\,n=9,\\,s=2.6\\,.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{8,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{8,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=9-1=8$ degrees of freedom. \n\n![](images/ce/ce7d5fc9bbf2c9e28a0862247e6f762cad8a718401828070e96041e7f82b1891.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{8,0.05}=1.860:$   \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $1.860$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $\\,3.355\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 25.5\\pm 1.860\\cdot \\frac{2.6}{\\sqrt{9}}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2} \\\\  & 25.5\\pm 1.612\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502151 :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=100$ from a population of size $N=500$ in which $90%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=60$ from a population of size $N=1\\,500$ in which $90%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=40$ from a population of size $N=1\\,500$ in which $90%$ of the population has a particular characteristic." "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=100$ from a population of size $N=500$ in which $90\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=60$ from a population of size $N=1\\,500$ in which $90\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=40$ from a population of size $N=1\\,500$ in which $90\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic."] [:db/cas 17592186502154 :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=75$ from a population where $45%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that less than $50%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=75$ from a population where $45\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that less than $50\\%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*"] [:db/cas 17592186502164 :question/problem "A dried apricot producer knows that $70%$ of apricots weigh more than $40$ g. If the factory randomly samples $350$ apricots, find a normal approximation for the probability that fewer than $224$ apricots weigh more than $40$ g. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" "A dried apricot producer knows that $70\\%$ of apricots weigh more than $40$ g. If the factory randomly samples $350$ apricots, find a normal approximation for the probability that fewer than $224$ apricots weigh more than $40$ g. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*"] [:db/cas 17592186503683 :question/worked-solution "We first draw a picture of the situation:\n\n![](images/41/417b284c87f735eb0c33f2369262b1548f9c54ff27ecbeb7880e91e9bc847f31.png)\n\nLet's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =12+l+\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $12,$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<12.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}} & =l \\\\ 144-{l}^{2} & ={l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =144 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =72 \\\\ l & =6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=6\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,6\\sqrt{2})$ | $6\\sqrt{2}$ | $(6\\sqrt{2},12)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $9$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=6\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6\\sqrt{2}) & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{144-(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{144-72} \\\\  & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{72} \\\\  & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+6\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =12+12\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =12(1+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We first draw a picture of the situation:\n\n![](images/41/417b284c87f735eb0c33f2369262b1548f9c54ff27ecbeb7880e91e9bc847f31.png)\n\nLet's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =12+l+\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $12,$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<12.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}} & =l \\\\ 144-{l}^{2} & ={l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =144 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =72 \\\\ l & =6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=6\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,6\\sqrt{2})$ | $6\\sqrt{2}$ | $(6\\sqrt{2},12)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $9$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=6\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6\\sqrt{2}) & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{144-(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{144-72} \\\\  & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{72} \\\\  & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+6\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =12+12\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =12(1+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503709 :question/problem "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $g(x)=\\frac{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{2x+1}.$" "Find the derivative of $g(x)=\\frac{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{2x+1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186505729 :question/problem "The painter's customer paid the painter $$247.63$ over $4$ days. Estimate the amount of money the customer paid the painter each day by rounding the total amount paid to the nearest dollar.\n\nThe customer paid approximately $\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ per day." "The painter's customer paid the painter $\\$247.63$ over $4$ days. Estimate the amount of money the customer paid the painter each day by rounding the total amount paid to the nearest dollar.\n\nThe customer paid approximately \\$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ per day."] [:db/cas 17592186505729 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the amount of money the customer paid for each day the painter worked, we need to find an approximation for $247.63÷4.$\n\nSo, we round the dividend to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n247.63\\to 248\n$$\n\nNow, we divide $248$ by $4$ as usual:\n\n$$\n248÷4=62\n$$\n\nTherefore, the customer paid approximately $$62$ per day." "To estimate the amount of money the customer paid for each day the painter worked, we need to find an approximation for $247.63÷4.$\n\nSo, we round the dividend to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n247.63\\to 248\n$$\n\nNow, we divide $248$ by $4$ as usual:\n\n$$\n248÷4=62\n$$\n\nTherefore, the customer paid approximately $\\$62$ per day."] [:db/cas 17592186505734 :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" "Evaluate $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186505735 :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}),$ for $m>0.$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" "Evaluate $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}),$ for $m>0.$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186505736 :question/problem "A survey of $1\\,200$ randomly selected U.S. households reveals that $816$ of them own at least one pet. Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. households that own at least one pet.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" "A survey of $1\\,200$ randomly selected U.S. households reveals that $816$ of them own at least one pet. Find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. households that own at least one pet.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186505736 :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all U.S. households,\n\n- some of the households surveyed own at least one pet, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=1\\,200,$ and\n\n- the proportion of households in the sample that own at least one pet can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p\\,}=\\frac{816}{1\\,200}=0.68\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=1\\,200\\cdot 0.68=816>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=1\\,200\\cdot (1-0.68)=384>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f5e154855d4a4f18fb81d522873fb29c11fd7c9543ffebdeac0f15672c7e72b2.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0,68\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.68(1-0.68)}{1\\,200}} \\\\  & 0.68\\pm 0.035\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all U.S. households,\n\n- some of the households surveyed own at least one pet, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=1\\,200,$ and\n\n- the proportion of households in the sample that own at least one pet can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p\\,}=\\frac{816}{1\\,200}=0.68\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=1\\,200\\cdot 0.68=816>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=1\\,200\\cdot (1-0.68)=384>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f5e154855d4a4f18fb81d522873fb29c11fd7c9543ffebdeac0f15672c7e72b2.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0,68\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.68(1-0.68)}{1\\,200}} \\\\  & 0.68\\pm 0.035\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505738 :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡3=$" "$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡3=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505760 :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡2\\sqrt{3}=$" "$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡2\\sqrt{3}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505767 :question/problem "The financial manager of a large company conducts a random sample of $120$ accounts receivable and finds that $42$ of them are more than $30$ days past due. Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all of the company's accounts receivable that are more than $30$ days past due. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" "The financial manager of a large company conducts a random sample of $120$ accounts receivable and finds that $42$ of them are more than $30$ days past due. Find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all of the company's accounts receivable that are more than $30$ days past due. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186505767 :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all accounts receivable from a certain company,\n\n- some of the accounts selected are more than $30$ days past due, and some are not,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=120,$ and\n\n- the proportion of accounts receivable in the sample that are more than $30$ days past due can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n\\widehat{p\\,}=\\frac{42}{120}=0.35\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=120\\cdot 0.35=42>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=120\\cdot (1-0.35)=78>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot 