\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505792 :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})=$" "$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505793 :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡6=$" "$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡6=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505798 :question/problem "![](images/68/6867cf1d5e64f785003037162c6e62d0531d6c4dd8330adf139f8ae8ac46082b.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=90$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.6,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" "![](images/68/6867cf1d5e64f785003037162c6e62d0531d6c4dd8330adf139f8ae8ac46082b.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=90$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.6,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*"] [:db/cas 17592186505798 :question/worked-solution "Since $n=90$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.6,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=90\\cdot 0.6=54>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=90\\cdot (1-0.6)=36>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.6\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.6(1-0.6)}{90}} \\\\  & 0.6\\pm 0.101\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $n=90$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.6,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=90\\cdot 0.6=54>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=90\\cdot (1-0.6)=36>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.6\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.6(1-0.6)}{90}} \\\\  & 0.6\\pm 0.101\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505802 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=140$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that $15%$ of the sample has the characteristic, find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" "Consider a sample of size $n=140$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that $15\\%$ of the sample has the characteristic, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*"] [:db/cas 17592186505802 :question/worked-solution "Since $n=140$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=15%=0.15,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=140\\cdot 0.15=21>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=140\\cdot (1-0.15)=119>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\n![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.15\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.15(1-0.15)}{140}} \\\\  & 0.15\\pm 0.050\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $n=140$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=15\\%=0.15,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=140\\cdot 0.15=21>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=140\\cdot (1-0.15)=119>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\n![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.15\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.15(1-0.15)}{140}} \\\\  & 0.15\\pm 0.050\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505806 :question/problem "$$\n\nGiven that $0<x<\\frac{1}{4},$ express $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1)$ using the definition of the inverse hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" "Given that $0<x<\\frac{1}{4},$ express $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1)$ using the definition of the inverse hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186505812 :question/problem "$$\n\nGiven that $-2<w<1,$ express $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3})$ using the definition of the inverse hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3})=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" "Given that $-2<w<1,$ express $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3})$ using the definition of the inverse hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3})=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186505831 :question/problem "![](images/68/6867cf1d5e64f785003037162c6e62d0531d6c4dd8330adf139f8ae8ac46082b.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=120$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.4,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" "![](images/68/6867cf1d5e64f785003037162c6e62d0531d6c4dd8330adf139f8ae8ac46082b.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=120$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.4,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*"] [:db/cas 17592186505831 :question/worked-solution "Since $n=120$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.4,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=120\\cdot 0.4=48>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=120\\cdot (1-0.4)=72>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.4\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.4(1-0.4)}{120}} \\\\  & 0.4\\pm 0.088\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $n=120$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.4,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=120\\cdot 0.4=48>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=120\\cdot (1-0.4)=72>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.4\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.4(1-0.4)}{120}} \\\\  & 0.4\\pm 0.088\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505836 :question/problem "A survey of $480$ randomly selected U.S. adults reveals that $336$ of the respondents have read at least one book in the past year. Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. adults who read at least one book in the past year.  \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" "A survey of $480$ randomly selected U.S. adults reveals that $336$ of the respondents have read at least one book in the past year. Find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. adults who read at least one book in the past year.  \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186505836 :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of  all U.S. adults,\n\n- some of the respondents read at least one book in the past year, and some didn't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=480,$ and\n\n- the proportion of adults who read at least one book in the past year can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p\\,}=\\frac{336}{480}=0.7\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=480\\cdot 0.7=336>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=480\\cdot (1-0.7)=144>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f58f278f66244732c379f3ad0f064227298915b8e7c364b91b8766bb8789c1ea.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.7\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{480}} \\\\  & 0.7\\pm 0.041\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of  all U.S. adults,\n\n- some of the respondents read at least one book in the past year, and some didn't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=480,$ and\n\n- the proportion of adults who read at least one book in the past year can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p\\,}=\\frac{336}{480}=0.7\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=480\\cdot 0.7=336>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=480\\cdot (1-0.7)=144>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f58f278f66244732c379f3ad0f064227298915b8e7c364b91b8766bb8789c1ea.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.7\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{480}} \\\\  & 0.7\\pm 0.041\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505844 :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=120$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.2,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" "![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=120$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.2,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*"] [:db/cas 17592186505844 :question/worked-solution "Since $n=120$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.2,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=120\\cdot 0.2=24>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=120\\cdot (1-0.2)=96>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.2\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.2(1-0.2)}{120}} \\\\  & 0.2\\pm 0.060\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $n=120$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.2,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\widehat{\\,p}=120\\cdot 0.2=24>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=120\\cdot (1-0.2)=96>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.2\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.2(1-0.2)}{120}} \\\\  & 0.2\\pm 0.060\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505845 :question/problem "$$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}),$ for $t>0?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}),$ for $t>0?$"] [:db/cas 17592186507444 :question/problem "The price of a deli sandwich is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $$1.10.$ In addition, in $90%$ of cases the price is between $$0.80\\mu $ and $$1.20\\mu .$ Find the average price, **rounding your answer to two decimal places**.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$\\mu ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$dollars$" "The price of a deli sandwich is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $\\$1.10.$ In addition, in $90\\%$ of cases the price is between $\\$0.80\\mu $ and $\\$1.20\\mu .$ Find the average price, **rounding your answer to two decimal places**.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$\\mu ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$dollars$"] [:db/cas 17592186507489 :question/problem "The heights of oak trees in a park are normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ feet and a standard deviation of $8$ feet. Additionally, $25%$ of the trees are shorter than $45$ feet. Find the percentage of trees taller than $50$ feet. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z).$*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4090\\,$ | $\\,0.4052\\,$ | $\\,0.4013\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4483\\,$ | $\\,0.4443\\,$ | $\\,0.4404\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.4880\\,$ | $\\,0.4840\\,$ | $\\,0.4801\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the trees are taller than $50$ feet." "The heights of oak trees in a park are normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ feet and a standard deviation of $8$ feet. Additionally, $25\\%$ of the trees are shorter than $45$ feet. Find the percentage of trees taller than $50$ feet. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z).$*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4090\\,$ | $\\,0.4052\\,$ | $\\,0.4013\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4483\\,$ | $\\,0.4443\\,$ | $\\,0.4404\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.4880\\,$ | $\\,0.4840\\,$ | $\\,0.4801\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the trees are taller than $50$ feet."] [:db/cas 17592186507489 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the height of a randomly selected tree. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{8}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>50).$  \n\nWe know that $P(X<45)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<45) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{45-\\mu }{8}) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{45-\\mu }{8}\\right) & =0.25 \\\\ \\frac{45-\\mu }{8} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)=-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{45-\\mu }{8} & =-0.6745 \\\\ 45-\\mu  & =-5.396 \\\\ \\mu  & =50.396.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(50<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>50) & =P(Z>\\frac{50-50.396}{8}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-0.05) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.05) \\\\  & =1-0.4801 \\\\  & ≈51.99%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $51.99%$ of the trees are taller than $50$ feet." "Let $X$ represent the height of a randomly selected tree. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{8}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>50).$  \n\nWe know that $P(X<45)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<45) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{45-\\mu }{8}) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{45-\\mu }{8}\\right) & =0.25 \\\\ \\frac{45-\\mu }{8} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)=-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{45-\\mu }{8} & =-0.6745 \\\\ 45-\\mu  & =-5.396 \\\\ \\mu  & =50.396.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(50<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>50) & =P(Z>\\frac{50-50.396}{8}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-0.05) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.05) \\\\  & =1-0.4801 \\\\  & ≈51.99\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $51.99\\%$ of the trees are taller than $50$ feet."] [:db/cas 17592186508074 :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{R(t)-R(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=2.$  Applying the above formula with $t=2$ and $h=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{R(2)-R(2-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{R(2)-R(1)}{1} \\\\  & =7\\,000-1\\,000 \\\\  & =6\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the rate of increase is $$6\\,000\\,/\\text{year}.$" "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{R(t)-R(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=2.$  Applying the above formula with $t=2$ and $h=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{R(2)-R(2-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{R(2)-R(1)}{1} \\\\  & =7\\,000-1\\,000 \\\\  & =6\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the rate of increase is $\\$6\\,000\\,/\\text{year}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186508915 :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab0416aeff61e4916acc1765a7931da0a41a64ba9d0ac4c05a322de6a1ed431b.png)\n\nA box whose depth $z$ is equal to half of its height $y$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the top and bottom costs $$4$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$1$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $36\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}} units" "![](images/ab/ab0416aeff61e4916acc1765a7931da0a41a64ba9d0ac4c05a322de6a1ed431b.png)\n\nA box whose depth $z$ is equal to half of its height $y$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the top and bottom costs $\\$4$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $\\$1$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $36\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}} units"] [:db/cas 17592186508920 :question/problem "Olivia bought a home for $$150\\,000.$ The value of the home declined by $16%$ annually. To the nearest dollar, what was the value of the home after $4$ years?" "Olivia bought a home for $\\$150\\,000.$ The value of the home declined by $16\\%$ annually. To the nearest dollar, what was the value of the home after $4$ years?"] [:db/cas 17592186508920 :question/worked-solution "The number $16%$ (expressed as a decimal) is equal to $0.16.$ Therefore, a decrease of $16%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of:\n\n$$\n1-0.16=0.84\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ year, value of the home was $150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, value of the home was $150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, value of the home was  $150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n- After $4$ years, value of the home was $150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)^{4}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the last expression gives:\n\n$$\n150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)^{4}≈74\\,681\n$$\n\nSo, the home was worth $$74\\,681$ (to the nearest dollar) after $4$ years." "The number $16\\%$ (expressed as a decimal) is equal to $0.16.$ Therefore, a decrease of $16\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of:\n\n$$\n1-0.16=0.84\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ year, value of the home was $150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, value of the home was $150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, value of the home was  $150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n- After $4$ years, value of the home was $150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)^{4}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the last expression gives:\n\n$$\n150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)^{4}≈74\\,681\n$$\n\nSo, the home was worth $\\$74\\,681$ (to the nearest dollar) after $4$ years."] [:db/cas 17592186508933 :question/problem "The daily milk production on Debbie's farm has decreased at a rate of $12%$ every month. If the daily milk production was $1\\,130$ liters initially, then which function correctly represents $p(t),$ the daily milk production after $t$ months?" "The daily milk production on Debbie's farm has decreased at a rate of $12\\%$ every month. If the daily milk production was $1\\,130$ liters initially, then which function correctly represents $p(t),$ the daily milk production after $t$ months?"] [:db/cas 17592186508933 :question/worked-solution "The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\np(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=1\\,130$ is the initial daily milk production.\n\n- $r=0.12$ is the decay rate ($12%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in months.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $p$ is\n\n$$\np(t)=1\\,130\\cdot (1-0.12)^{t}=1\\,130\\cdot (0.88)^{t}.\n$$" "The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\np(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=1\\,130$ is the initial daily milk production.\n\n- $r=0.12$ is the decay rate ($12\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in months.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $p$ is\n\n$$\np(t)=1\\,130\\cdot (1-0.12)^{t}=1\\,130\\cdot (0.88)^{t}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508935 :question/problem "![](images/85/8548c87f15edc7e7c903c753767f12bd6f4c6f0294ff07734659583907f2ea4a.png)\n\nA box whose width $x$ is $2$ times its depth $z$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the top and bottom costs $$5$ per square foot, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$2$ per square foot. The box must have a volume of $180\\,{ft}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}} units" "![](images/85/8548c87f15edc7e7c903c753767f12bd6f4c6f0294ff07734659583907f2ea4a.png)\n\nA box whose width $x$ is $2$ times its depth $z$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the top and bottom costs $\\$5$ per square foot, and the material for the lateral faces costs $\\$2$ per square foot. The box must have a volume of $180\\,{ft}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}} units"] [:db/cas 17592186508944 :question/problem "The population of a certain animal species is declining at a rate of $3%$ per year. If the species' initial population was $5\\,000$ when the observations started, then which function correctly represents $y(t),$ the population of the species after $t$ years?" "The population of a certain animal species is declining at a rate of $3\\%$ per year. If the species' initial population was $5\\,000$ when the observations started, then which function correctly represents $y(t),$ the population of the species after $t$ years?"] [:db/cas 17592186508944 :question/worked-solution "The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=5\\,000$ is the initial population.\n\n- $r=0.03$ is the decay rate ($3%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=5\\,000\\cdot (1-0.03)^{t}=5\\,000\\cdot (0.97)^{t}.\n$$" "The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=5\\,000$ is the initial population.\n\n- $r=0.03$ is the decay rate ($3\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=5\\,000\\cdot (1-0.03)^{t}=5\\,000\\cdot (0.97)^{t}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508947 :question/worked-solution "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1-r)^{t},$ where:\n\n- $a=20\\,000$ represents the initial number of ticks.\n\n- $1-r=0.7,$ where $r$ is the decay rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in weeks, since the acaricides were first applied.\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1-r=0.7\\,⟹\\,r=0.3.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.3$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $30%.$ \n\nSo, the base of the exponential expression $0.7$ means that the population of ticks decreases by $30%$ every week." "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1-r)^{t},$ where:\n\n- $a=20\\,000$ represents the initial number of ticks.\n\n- $1-r=0.7,$ where $r$ is the decay rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in weeks, since the acaricides were first applied.\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1-r=0.7\\,⟹\\,r=0.3.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.3$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $30\\%.$ \n\nSo, the base of the exponential expression $0.7$ means that the population of ticks decreases by $30\\%$ every week."] [:db/cas 17592186508951 :question/worked-solution "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1-r)^{t},$ where:\n\n- $a=500$ represents the initial number of customers.\n\n- $1-r=0.83,$ where $r$ is the decay rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time in days since the local news reported that the cafe did not pass a health inspection.\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1-r=0.83\\,⟹\\,r=0.17.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.17$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $17%.$ \n\nSo, the base of the exponential expression $0.83$ means that the number of customers decreases by $17%$ every day." "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1-r)^{t},$ where:\n\n- $a=500$ represents the initial number of customers.\n\n- $1-r=0.83,$ where $r$ is the decay rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time in days since the local news reported that the cafe did not pass a health inspection.\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1-r=0.83\\,⟹\\,r=0.17.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.17$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $17\\%.$ \n\nSo, the base of the exponential expression $0.83$ means that the number of customers decreases by $17\\%$ every day."] [:db/cas 17592186508964 :question/problem "The price of a new bicycle is $$625.$ If the bicycle loses $\\frac{1}{5}$ of its value every year, what will be its value after $4$ years?" "The price of a new bicycle is $\\$625.$ If the bicycle loses $\\frac{1}{5}$ of its value every year, what will be its value after $4$ years?"] [:db/cas 17592186508964 :question/worked-solution "Since the value of the bicycle reduces by $\\frac{1}{5}$ per year, $\\frac{4}{5}$ of its value remains every year. \n\nLet's calculate the value of the bicycle after every year:\n\n- After $1$ year, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{3}$ dollars.\n\n- After $4$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{4}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the last expression, we get\n\n$$\n625\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{4}=256.\n$$\n\nSo after $4$ years, the bicycle will be worth $$256.$" "Since the value of the bicycle reduces by $\\frac{1}{5}$ per year, $\\frac{4}{5}$ of its value remains every year. \n\nLet's calculate the value of the bicycle after every year:\n\n- After $1$ year, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{3}$ dollars.\n\n- After $4$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{4}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the last expression, we get\n\n$$\n625\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{4}=256.\n$$\n\nSo after $4$ years, the bicycle will be worth $\\$256.$"] [:db/cas 17592186508978 :question/problem "![](images/a9/a9de332f316c91f9739208d11828f6a0f7d035589dc4f3adc921e5108453cc12.png)\n\nA box with no lid and a square base has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the base costs $$5$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$2$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $10\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $x$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} units" "![](images/a9/a9de332f316c91f9739208d11828f6a0f7d035589dc4f3adc921e5108453cc12.png)\n\nA box with no lid and a square base has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the base costs $\\$5$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $\\$2$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $10\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $x$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} units"] [:db/cas 17592186508980 :question/worked-solution "The value $p(4)$ represents the value of $p(t)$ at $t=4.$ In this context, it represents the value of the boat $4$ years after it was purchased.\n\nSubstituting $t=4$ into the expression for $p(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(4) & =10\\,000\\cdot {(0.9)}^{4} \\\\  & =10\\,000\\cdot (0.656\\,1) \\\\  & =6\\,561.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the boat after four years is $$6,561.$" "The value $p(4)$ represents the value of $p(t)$ at $t=4.$ In this context, it represents the value of the boat $4$ years after it was purchased.\n\nSubstituting $t=4$ into the expression for $p(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(4) & =10\\,000\\cdot {(0.9)}^{4} \\\\  & =10\\,000\\cdot (0.656\\,1) \\\\  & =6\\,561.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the boat after four years is $\\$6,561.$"] [:db/cas 17592186508983 :question/problem "An office chair is marked down by $15%$ during a promotion. What is the sale price of the chair if its original price is $$85?$" "An office chair is marked down by $15\\%$ during a promotion. What is the sale price of the chair if its original price is $\\$85?$"]]
